  LegalTerm(term: 'Lis Pendens', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'A pending suit. The doctrine that property involved in litigation cannot be transferred to defeat the outcome.', usage: 'The buyer of property during lis pendens is bound by the decree as if he were a party.', section: 'Sec 52 Transfer of Property Act'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Non Est Factum', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'It is not his deed. A plea that a signed document is fundamentally different from what was understood.', usage: 'The illiterate farmer raised non est factum, claiming the sale deed was read to him as a lease agreement.'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Consensus Ad Idem', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'Meeting of minds. Both parties must agree on the same thing in the same sense for a valid contract.', usage: 'There was no consensus ad idem as the buyer intended immovable property but the seller meant movable property.'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Pacta Sunt Servanda', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'Agreements must be kept. Parties are bound by the terms of their contract.', usage: 'Under the principle of pacta sunt servanda, both nations must honour the terms of the bilateral treaty.'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Jus Cogens', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'Compelling law. Peremptory norms of international law from which no derogation is permitted.', usage: 'The prohibition against genocide is a jus cogens norm — no treaty or custom can override it.'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Compos Mentis', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'Of sound mind. Having full mental capacity.', usage: 'The testator was compos mentis when he signed the will, as attested by the doctor\'s certificate.'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Non Compos Mentis', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'Not of sound mind. Lacking mental capacity.', usage: 'The contract was voidable as the promisor was non compos mentis at the time of signing.', section: 'Sec 12 Indian Contract Act'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Corpus Delicti', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'Body of the crime. The factual evidence that a crime has occurred.', usage: 'Prosecution must establish the corpus delicti — in a murder case, typically the body or evidence of death.'),
  LegalTerm(term: 'Laches', category: 'Latin', meaning: 'Unreasonable delay. A defence based on the plaintiff\'s excessive delay in pursuing their claim.', usage: 'The writ petition was dismissed on the ground of laches as the petitioner waited 5 years to challenge the order.'),
